  "text": "The paper first surveys many experimental facts that any explanation must cover, then proposes a fresh view of how tiny new grains appear when a heavily deformed metal is heated. It is suggested that these grains start in the most strained regions of the crystal lattice, where the lattice bends sharply. In those spots the lattice can locally restore order through a recently observed rearrangement of line defects, creating a perfectly ordered region that can then grow by consuming the surrounding imperfect material. Evidence supporting this picture is presented. Each possible grain is assumed to have a specific waiting time before it becomes active, rather than a constant chance of activation as earlier models assumed. By assuming a certain spread of lattice curvatures and linking waiting time to curvature, a quantitative model is built that matches all measured nucleation rates. The work ends with a critical comparison to earlier theories."
